    "result_text": "The full suite passes (`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class; `AcceptanceBase`/`AuditLogCapture` are support classes).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented the email-uniqueness rule so creating an owner whose lower-cased email already belongs to another owner returns **409 Conflict**.\n\nFollowing the existing per-rule policy pattern in this codebase, I kept structural impact low by adding behaviour as small cohesive units rather than growing any existing method:\n\n1. **New `OwnerEmailPolicy`** (`service/OwnerEmailPolicy.java`) \u2014 a self-contained policy mirroring `OwnerTelephonePolicy`: `rejectDuplicateEmail(...)` compares the owner's lower-cased email against all existing owners and throws a `DuplicateEmailException`. Each method is trivial (CC \u2248 1) with no surrounding class complexity.\n\n2. **One line added to `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 `OwnerEmailPolicy.rejectDuplicateEmail(...)`, alongside the other create-flow policy calls (plus its import).\n\n3. **One new handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 a small `@ExceptionHandler` mapping `DuplicateEmailException` to 409, matching the existing telephone/household/city handlers (the catch-all `Exception` handler otherwise overrides `@ResponseStatus` and returns 500).\n\nEmails are already lower-cased at the mapper layer (`normalizeEmail`), so the stored/compared form is canonical. Each changed function stays simple, and no existing method or class was grown.",
